    "abstract": "Predicting an unmeasured intervention pair from measured single effects is a narrower task than general perturbation prediction, and improvement over additivity need not establish pair-specific interaction learning. We present Opal, an observed-single population-mean benchmark and family of symmetric residual predictors, with shared-distortion controls, technical measurement diagnostics, and separate development and confirmation records. Historical neural models reduce mean squared error over additivity by 15.81% on Norman and 61.08% on Wessels, but their advantage over stronger same-information references is inconclusive or negative. Exploratory nested development favors residual kernels over the tested neural additions, and increased pair coverage is more useful than the tested increase in single coverage. An EmeraldBay chemical pilot has kernel MSE 0.172218 versus additive 0.210239 and adapted aggregate-input CPA 0.157098. An independent D-SPIN evaluation refits the frozen EmeraldBay-selected procedure on source contexts while withholding two lineages across batches. Its MSE is 0.034025 versus 0.033108 for a source-selected pooled-pair kernel, failing the registered superiority gate. A nominal 90% empirical source radius covers 34.3% of held conditions. In separate baseline-only development, tested learned state models are 13.91% and 22.65% worse than simple references on EmeraldBay and sci-Plex 3. Pair experiments also show substantial cancellation between main-effect and residual errors. A separately registered successor batch finds no new context, pair-kernel or neural component that passes its five-percent development gate; its best identity feature gain is 4.06%, and its shared neural corrections lose to matched simple references by 12.79% and 15.77% on Norman and Wessels. These results support a reproducible comparative research framework and strong classical references, while leaving robust biological interaction learning, positive independent transfer, and reliable out-of-distribution uncertainty unestablished.",
    "title": "Opal: an observed-single benchmark for combinatorial perturbation prediction",
    "authors": [
      "Arjun Vijay Prakash"
    ],
